{{define "about"}}{{template "head" .}}
<h1>About this catalog</h1>
<p>A static snapshot of what <code>.ai-rulez/</code> defines, generated by <code>ai-rulez catalog --html</code>. It has no server, makes no network request and opens from <code>file://</code>.</p>
<table>
<caption>Provenance</caption>
<tbody>
<tr><th scope="row">Tool</th><td>{{s .Doc.GeneratedBy.Name}} {{s .Doc.GeneratedBy.Version}}</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Schema version</th><td>{{.SchemaVersion}}</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Catalog digest</th><td><code class="digest">{{s .Digest}}</code></td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Tokenizer</th><td>{{s .Doc.Tokenizer}}</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<h2>Verify it</h2>
<p>The output is reproducible: run <code>ai-rulez catalog --html</code> into a clean directory with the same sources and compare the trees, or compare the catalog digest above with the sha256 of <a href="catalog.json">catalog.json</a>.</p>
<h2>Handle it like the repository</h2>
<p>This site lists skill names, descriptions, owners, token costs and lint findings, so it is as sensitive as the configuration directory it describes. Crawlers are told to stay away unless it was built with <code>--indexable</code>.</p>
<p>Each page carries a strict Content-Security-Policy in a meta tag. A host should also send equivalent response headers, including <code>frame-ancestors 'none'</code>, which a meta tag cannot set.</p>
<h2>Schema</h2>
<p>The data behind every page is <a href="catalog.json">catalog.json</a> (schema version {{.SchemaVersion}}).</p>
